# SQLHeal: Clause-level Diagnosis and Targeted Self-Healing for Text-to-SQL

SQLHeal provides a modular framework for evaluating, diagnosing, and repairing text-to-SQL queries. This repository contains the Phase 1 Baseline implementation (B0 and B1) evaluated on BIRD dev alongside clause canonicalisation, SQLite execution sandboxing, and fault injection.

## Repository Structure

```text
sqlheal/
  README.md             install, data download, how to run B0 and B1
  pyproject.toml        dependencies (sqlglot, openai-compatible client, pytest)
  data/                 BIRD dev (SQLite), stress set, fault-injected set (git-ignored)
  src/sqlheal/
    parsing.py          sqlglot parse, clause table, canonical forms
    sandbox.py          read-only SQLite execution, typed state
    state.py            query state contract (pydantic models)
    llm.py              provider layer, caching, retries
    baselines/          b0_single.py, b1_fixer.py
    modules/            patcher.py, prober.py, verifier.py (Phase 2)
    faults.py           fault injector F1-F8
  eval/                 run_bird.py, metrics.py, report.py
  tests/                parser, sandbox, fault injector, metric unit tests
  configs/              model and budget settings
```

## Installation

Requirements: Python >= 3.10

```bash
# Create and activate virtual environment
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate

# Install package in editable mode with dev dependencies
pip install -e .
pip install pytest
```

## Download BIRD dev Data

BIRD dev SQLite databases and dev questions can be downloaded using the included script:

```bash
# Automated download and preparation
python eval/download_bird.py
```

Alternatively, manually place BIRD dev SQLite databases and `dev.json` into `data/bird/`:
```text
data/
  bird/
    dev.json
    dev_databases/
      <db_id>/
        <db_id>.sqlite
```

## How to Run B0 and B1

### Configuration
Set your LLM API configuration via environment variables:
```bash
export OPENAI_API_KEY="your-api-key"
export OPENAI_BASE_URL="https://api.openai.com/v1" # or local vLLM / Ollama endpoint
```

Model and budget settings are specified in `configs/default.json`:
```json
{
  "model": "gpt-4o-mini",
  "temperature": 0.0,
  "max_repair_turns": 2,
  "timeout_sec": 30.0
}
```

### Running B0 (Single Turn Zero-shot)
```bash
python eval/run_bird.py --system b0 --config configs/default.json --split dev --output_dir results/b0
```

### Running B1 (Iterative Whole-Query Regeneration Fixer)
```bash
python eval/run_bird.py --system b1 --config configs/default.json --split dev --output_dir results/b1
```

### Generating Comparison Report
```bash
python eval/report.py --b0 results/b0/results.json --b1 results/b1/results.json
```

## Phase 1 Baseline Results

The table below reports initial baseline results across BIRD dev (1,534 questions):

| Model | System | ERROR rate (%) | EMPTY rate (%) | EX (%) | Questions |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| GPT-4o-mini | B0 | 14.8 | 8.2 | 52.4 | 1,534 |
| GPT-4o-mini | B1 | 6.5 | 5.1 | 58.9 | 1,534 |
| Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct | B0 | 22.4 | 12.1 | 41.2 | 1,534 |
| Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct | B1 | 11.8 | 8.7 | 46.5 | 1,534 |

## Running Tests

All unit tests verify clause canonicalisation, read-only sandboxing, fault injection (F1-F8), and metric definitions:
```bash
pytest
```
